+++
title: Babylon
description: Ancient Mesopotamian city in Iraq
hook: Babylon was perhaps the first city to reach a population above 200,000.
  The main sources for it — excavation of the site itself, cuneiform texts,
  the Bible, Herodotus and other classical authors — present an incomplete and
  sometimes contradictory picture, even at its peak in the sixth century BC.
standfirst: An ancient city on the lower Euphrates in southern Mesopotamia, in
  modern-day Hillah, Iraq. Its rulers established two important empires, the
  19th–16th century BC Old Babylonian and the 7th–6th century BC
  Neo-Babylonian, and it was one of the most important urban centres of the
  ancient Near East until its decline during the Hellenistic period.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Hillah, Babil Governorate, Iraq
  - name: Built
    value: c. 2200 BC
  - name: Abandoned
    value: c. 1000 AD
  - name: Area
    value: 9 km2 (3.5 sq mi)
  - name: Condition
    value: Ruined
  - name: World Heritage Site
    value: "Designated 2019 (43rd session), Cultural: (iii), (vi)"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Subsequently he marched to Babylon, and he destroyed Babylon, and
      defeated the Hurrian troops, and brought captives and possessions of
      Babylon to Hattusa.
    attribution: Telepinu Proclamation
    section: middle-babylon
  - text: "Behold, the days come, that all that is in thine house, and that which
      thy fathers have laid up in store unto this day, shall be carried into
      Babylon: nothing shall be left, saith the LORD"
    attribution: the prophet Isaiah, to Hezekiah (2 Kings 20:12 and Isaiah 39)
    section: biblical-narrative
  - text: "And Babylon, the glory of kingdoms, the beauty of the Chaldees'
      excellency, shall be as when God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah. It shall
      never be inhabited, neither shall it be dwelt in from generation to
      generation: neither shall the Arabian pitch tent there; neither shall
      the shepherds make their fold there."
    attribution: Isaiah 13:19
    section: biblical-narrative
  - text: "I was completely deceived: instead of a few insulated mounds, I found
      the whole face of the country covered with vestiges of building, in some
      places consisting of brick walls surprisingly fresh, in others merely of
      a vast succession of mounds of rubbish of such indeterminate figures,
      variety and extent, as to involve the person who should have formed any
      theory in inextricable confusion."
    attribution: Claudius J. Rich, Memoir on the Ruins of Babylon (1815), pp. 1–2
    section: archaeology
  - text: They caused substantial damage to the Ishtar Gate, one of the most
      famous monuments from antiquity [...] US military vehicles crushed
      2,600-year-old brick pavements, archaeological fragments were scattered
      across the site, more than 12 trenches were driven into ancient deposits
      and military earth-moving projects contaminated the site for future
      generations of scientists.
    attribution: John Curtis, in a report of the British Museum's Near East department
    section: under-us-and-polish-occupation
  - text: This was built by Saddam Hussein, son of Nebuchadnezzar, to glorify Iraq.
    attribution: a frequent inscription on bricks laid during Saddam Hussein's
      restoration project
    section: iraqi-government
timeline:
  - when: 3rd millennium BC
    what: Babylon in existence no later than this, by the Wikidata record of its
      inception.
    from: |-
      inception:
        value: 3rd millennium BC
        sourcing: no later than
  - when: c. 2200 BC
    what: The date given for Babylon's building.
    from: |-
      history:
        built: c. 2200 BC
  - when: 2217–2193 BC
    what: The earliest known mention of Babylon, as a small town, on a clay tablet
      from the reign of Shar-Kali-Sharri of the Akkadian Empire.
    from: The earliest known mention of Babylon as a small town appears on a clay
      tablet from the reign of Shar-Kali-Sharri (2217–2193 BC), of the
      Akkadian Empire.
  - when: 1894 BC – 1792 BC
    what: Babylon recorded as a city-state.
    from: |-
      instance-of:
        - value: city-state
          from: 1894 BC
          to: 1792 BC
  - when: 1792–1750 BC
    what: The reign of Hammurabi, who codified the laws of Babylonia into the Code
      of Hammurabi and ruled southern Mesopotamia from Babylon.
    from: Hammurabi (r. 1792–1750 BC) is famous for codifying the laws of
      Babylonia into the Code of Hammurabi.
  - when: 1595 BC
    what: Babylon is thought to have been sacked by Mursili I, ruler of the
      Hittite Empire.
    from: In 1595 BC, the city is thought to have been sacked by Mursili I, ruler
      of the Hittite Empire sometime during the 31-year reign of Samsu-Ditana,
      last ruler of the First Dynasty of Babylon.
  - when: 1160 BC
    what: The end of the 435-year Kassite dynasty, which had renamed the city
      Karduniash.
    from: Thereafter, the Kassite dynasty took power in the city of Babylon,
      renaming it Karduniash, ushering in a dynasty that lasted for 435 years,
      until 1160 BC.
  - when: 689 BC
    what: Sennacherib razes Babylon's walls, temples and palaces, and the rubble
      is thrown into the Arakhtu.
    from: In 689 BC, its walls, temples, and palaces were razed, and the rubble
      was thrown into the Arakhtu, the sea bordering the earlier Babylon to
      the south.
  - when: 652 BC
    what: Shamash-shum-ukin starts a civil war against his brother Ashurbanipal,
      who ruled in Nineveh.
    from: After his death, Babylonia was governed by his elder son, the Assyrian
      prince Shamash-shum-ukin, who eventually started a civil war in 652 BC
      against his own brother, Ashurbanipal, who ruled in Nineveh.
  - when: 626 to 539 BC
    what: Babylon is capital of the short-lived Neo-Babylonian Empire.
    from: After the Assyrians destroyed and then rebuilt it, Babylon became the
      capital of the short-lived Neo-Babylonian Empire, from 626 to 539 BC.
  - when: 539 BC
    what: The Neo-Babylonian Empire falls to Cyrus the Great at the Battle of Opis.
    from: In 539 BC, the Neo-Babylonian Empire fell to Cyrus the Great, the king
      of Persia, with a military engagement known as the Battle of Opis.
  - when: October of 331 BC
    what: Darius III is defeated at the Battle of Gaugamela and Alexander the
      Great occupies Babylon.
    from: In October of 331 BC, Darius III, the last Achaemenid king of the
      Persian Empire, was defeated by the forces of the Ancient Macedonian
      ruler Alexander the Great at the Battle of Gaugamela, occupying Babylon.
  - when: 323 BC
    what: Alexander dies in the palace of Nebuchadnezzar; his empire is divided
      among the Diadochi.
    from: However, following Alexander's death in 323 BC in the palace of
      Nebuchadnezzar, his empire was divided amongst his generals, the
      Diadochi, and decades of fighting soon began.
  - when: AD 116
    what: Trajan briefly captures Babylon for the newly conquered Roman province
      of Mesopotamia.
    from: Although it was captured briefly by Trajan in AD 116 to be part of the
      newly conquered province of Mesopotamia, his successor, Hadrian,
      relinquished his conquests east of the Euphrates river, which became
      again the Roman Empire's eastern boundary.
  - when: mid-7th century
    what: Mesopotamia is conquered and settled by the expanding Muslim empire, and
      Babylon is dissolved as a province.
    from: In the mid-7th century, Mesopotamia was conquered and settled by the
      expanding Muslim empire, and a period of Islamization followed. Babylon
      was dissolved as a province, and Aramaic and Church of the East
      Christianity eventually became marginalized.
  - when: 11th century AD
    what: The last known habitation of the town, then called the "small village of
      Babel".
    from: The last known habitation of the town dates from the 11th century AD,
      when it was referred to as the "small village of Babel".
  - when: 1811–12 and 1817
    what: Claudius Rich, working for the British East India Company in Baghdad,
      excavates Babylon.
    from: Claudius Rich, working for the British East India Company in Baghdad,
      excavated Babylon in 1811–12 and again in 1817.
  - when: 1899 until 1917
    what: Robert Koldewey's German Oriental Society team runs the first scientific
      excavations at Babylon, working daily.
    from: A team from the German Oriental Society led by Robert Koldewey conducted
      the first scientific archaeological excavations at Babylon. The work was
      conducted daily from 1899 until 1917.
  - when: February 1978
    what: Saddam Hussein's Ba'athist government begins the "Archaeological
      Restoration of Babylon Project", rebuilding features of the ancient city
      atop its ruins.
    from: "In February 1978, the Ba'athist government of Iraq under Saddam Hussein
      began the \"Archaeological Restoration of Babylon Project\":
      reconstructing features of the ancient city atop its ruins."
  - when: September 2003
    what: The area around Babylon passes from US troops to Polish forces after the
      2003 invasion of Iraq.
    from: Following the 2003 invasion of Iraq, the area around Babylon came under
      the control of US troops, before being handed over to Polish forces in
      September 2003.
  - when: May 2009
    what: The provincial government of Babil reopens the site to tourists.
    from: In May 2009, the provincial government of Babil reopened the site to
      tourists.
  - when: July 2019
    what: Babylon is inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.
    from: In July 2019, the site of Babylon was inscribed as a UNESCO World
      Heritage Site.
image-plan:
  - file: Ishtar_Gate_at_Berlin_Museum.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The blue-tiled Ishtar Gate as reconstructed in the Pergamon Museum,
      Berlin. It was the northern entrance to Babylon, named for the goddess
      of love and war, and decorated with bulls and dragons, symbols of the
      god Marduk.
  - file: Map_of_Babylon_with_major_areas.jpg
    role: inline
    section: names
    caption: The major areas of Babylon, with the modern-day villages that sit
      among them.
  - file: Babylon,_1932.jpg
    role: inline
    section: names
    caption: Babylon in 1932.
  - file: Plan_of_Ruins_of_Babylon_1905.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: A 1905 map of the ruins, with the locations and names of the villages
      then standing on them.
  - file: Confluence_of_the_Tigris_and_Euphrates_near_Al-Qurnah.tif
    role: inline
    section: excavations
    caption: Near Al-Qurnah, where the Qurnah Disaster of 1855 sent hundreds of
      cases of antiquities from Fresnel's mission to the bottom of the Tigris.
  - file: Original_tiles_of_the_processional_street._Ancient_Babylon,_Mesopotamia,_Iraq..jpg
    role: inline
    section: excavations
    caption: Original tiles of the processional street, the route Koldewey's dig
      followed up to the temple of Marduk.
  - file: Mušḫuššu_(sirrush)_and_aurochs_on_either_side_of_the_processional_street._Ancient_Babylon,_Mesopotamia,_Iraq.jpg
    role: inline
    section: excavations
    caption: Mušḫuššu (sirrush) and aurochs facing each other across the
      processional street.
  - file: Dynastic_Chronicle.jpg
    role: drop
    section: history
    caption: Leonard William King's illustration of fragment K. 8532, part of the
      Dynastic Chronicle listing rulers of Babylon grouped by dynasty.
  - file: Ancient_Remains_in_Babylon.jpg
    role: drop
    section: present-day
    caption: Brick structures in Babylon, 2016.
  - file: Relieve_Reina_de_la_Noche_(ca._1800_a.C).jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: The Queen of the Night relief. The figure could be an aspect of
      Ishtar, Babylonian goddess of sex and love.
  - file: Hammurabi's_Babylonia_1.svg
    role: inline
    section: old-babylonian-period
    caption: "What Hammurabi inherited in 1792 BC and what he left in 1750 BC: the
      Babylonian territory at his ascension and at his death."
  - file: Cylinder_Seal,_Old_Babylonian,_formerly_in_the_Charterhouse_Collection_03.jpg
    role: inline
    section: old-babylonian-period
    caption: An Old Babylonian hematite cylinder seal, probably made at Sippar
      during or shortly before Hammurabi's reign. It shows the king making an
      animal offering to the sun god Shamash.
  - file: Cylinder_Seal,_Old_Babylonian,_formerly_in_the_Charterhouse_Collection_09.jpg
    role: drop
    section: old-babylonian-period
    caption: Linescan camera image of the same seal, reversed to resemble an
      impression.
  - file: Sennacherib.jpg
    role: inline
    section: assyrian-period
    caption: Sennacherib during his Babylonian war, on a relief from his palace at
      Nineveh. His answer to Babylonia's revolt was to raze the city in 689
      BC.
  - file: Cuneiform_cylinder-_inscription_of_Nebuchadnezzar_II_commemorating_the_reconstruction_of_Etemenanki,_the_ziggurat_at_Babylon_MET_ME86_11_284.jpg
    role: inline
    section: neo-babylonian-empire
    caption: A cuneiform cylinder from the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II, honoring
      the exorcism and reconstruction of the ziggurat Etemenanki by
      Nabopolassar.
  - file: Babylon_relief.jpg
    role: inline
    section: neo-babylonian-empire
    caption: Detail of a relief from the reconstructed Ishtar Gate in the Pergamon
      Museum, Berlin.
  - file: Xerxes_I_tomb_Babylonian_soldier_circa_470_BCE.jpg
    role: inline
    section: persian-conquest
    caption: A Babylonian soldier in the Achaemenid army, c. 470 BC, from the tomb
      of Xerxes I.
  - file: Charles_Le_Brun_-_Entry_of_Alexander_into_Babylon.JPG
    role: full
    section: hellenistic-period
    caption: Charles LeBrun's "Entry of Alexander into Babylon" (1665) shows
      Alexander the Great's uncontested entry into the city, envisioned with
      pre-existing Hellenistic architecture.
  - file: مـدينة_بابل_03.jpg
    role: inline
    section: muslim-conquest
    caption: Ruins of Babylon. Ibn Hawqal and al-Qazwini found only a small
      village here.
  - file: أسد_بابل_-_Lion_of_Babylon.png
