  - file: Flag_of_Iraq.svg
    role: drop
    section: iraqi-government
    caption: Flag of Iraq.
glossary:
  - term: ensi
    gloss: The governor who ruled Babylon on the Akkadian empire's behalf; Abba,
      Arši-aḫ, Itūr-ilum, Murteli, Unabatal and Puzur-Tutu are among the known
      holders.
    from: Babylon was ruled by an ensi (governor) for the empire. Some of the
      known governors were Abba, Arši-aḫ, Itūr-ilum, Murteli, Unabatal, and
      Puzur-Tutu.
  - term: Etemenanki
    gloss: The ziggurat of Nebuchadnezzar II, at the Kasr mound in the centre of
      the site; its remains were heaped on the Homera mound when Alexander the
      Great demolished it in 331 BC.
    from: Kasr – also called Palace or Castle, it was the location of the
      Neo-Babylonian ziggurat Etemenanki of Nebuchadnezzar II. It lies in the
      center of the site and rises to 19 meters (62ft) above the plain. [...]
      Homera – a reddish-colored mound on the west side. Most of the
      Hellenistic remains are here. Most of the remains of the ziggurat
      Etemenanki were heaped here when it was demolished by Alexander the
      Great in 331 BC.
  - term: Esagila
    gloss: A temple of Marduk at the Amran Ibn Ali mound, which also contained
      shrines to Ea and Nabu.
    from: Amran Ibn Ali – about 22 meters (72ft) high and at the south of the
      site. It is the site of Esagila, a temple of Marduk that also contained
      shrines to Ea and Nabu.
  - term: Karduniash
    gloss: The name the Kassite dynasty gave Babylon when it took power there, for
      a dynasty that lasted 435 years, until 1160 BC.
    from: Thereafter, the Kassite dynasty took power in the city of Babylon,
      renaming it Karduniash, ushering in a dynasty that lasted for 435 years,
      until 1160 BC.
  - term: Determinative
    gloss: A cuneiform sign that tells you how to read the signs before it — the
      KI at the end of KÁ.DIG̃IR.RAKI marks them as a place name.
    from: The final 𒆠 (KI) is a determinative indicating that the previous signs
      are to be understood as a place name.
  - term: Folk etymology
    gloss: 'A meaning read into a name after the fact: "gate of god" is
      increasingly viewed as a Semitic one, attached to an unknown original
      non-Semitic placename.'
    from: However, the "gate of god" interpretation is increasingly viewed as a
      Semitic folk etymology to explain an unknown original non-Semitic
      placename.
  - term: Diadochi
    gloss: Alexander the Great's generals, among whom his empire was divided after
      he died in 323 BC, and who then fought each other for decades.
    from: However, following Alexander's death in 323 BC in the palace of
      Nebuchadnezzar, his empire was divided amongst his generals, the
      Diadochi, and decades of fighting soon began.
  - term: Qurnah Disaster
    gloss: The May 1855 sinking of a transport ship and four rafts on the Tigris
      near Al-Qurnah, after an attack by river pirates, carrying over 200
      crates of excavated artifacts.
    from: Much of their work was lost in the Qurnah Disaster, when a transport
      ship and four rafts sank on the Tigris river in May 1855. They had been
      carrying over 200 crates of artifacts from various excavation missions,
      when they were attacked by Tigris river pirates near Al-Qurnah.
further:
  - slug: hanging-gardens-of-babylon
    why: Ranked among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, credited to
      Nebuchadnezzar II — and not mentioned in any extant Babylonian text.
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: For 911–609 BC Babylonia was under constant Assyrian domination or direct
      control, and in 689 BC Sennacherib razed the city entirely.
  - slug: akkadian-empire
    why: Babylon's first appearance in the record is as a town of this empire, in
      a year name of Shar-Kali-Sharri.
anchors:
  hook: It was perhaps the first city to reach a population above 200,000 in an
    area of about 900 ha (3.5 sq mi). The main sources of information about
    Babylon—excavation of the site itself, references in cuneiform texts found
    elsewhere in Mesopotamia, references in the Bible, descriptions in
    Herodotus and other classical authors, and second-hand summaries of the
    work of Ctesias and Berossus—present an incomplete and sometimes
    contradictory picture of the ancient city, even at its peak in the sixth
    century BC.
  standfirst: Babylon was an ancient city located on the lower Euphrates river
    in southern Mesopotamia, within modern-day Hillah, Iraq, about 85
    kilometres (53 miles) south of modern-day Baghdad. Babylon functioned as
    the main cultural and political centre of the Akkadian-speaking region of
    Babylonia. Its rulers established two important empires in antiquity, the
    19th–16th century BC Old Babylonian Empire, and the 7th–6th century BC
    Neo-Babylonian Empire. Babylon also served as a regional capital of other
    empires, such as the Achaemenid Empire. Babylon was one of the most
    important urban centres of the ancient Near East until its decline during
    the Hellenistic period.
